; ir_sirc_rx.loom
; Sony SIRC infrared receiver (12, 15 and 20 bit) on IN0, for the output of a
; demodulating receiver module (a TSOP38240 or alike: low while a 40 kHz
; burst is present), delivering frames and errors to the host through OUTQ[0].
;
; Written on 2026-11-05, after the hardware was complete (D-032), as a
; protocol Loom was not designed for (D-035): no design document names SIRC.
; Nothing in Loom was built for infrared. As ir_nec_rx.loom does, the program
; measures every mark and space with the timed waits (`WAITP ... T` against a
; `SETD` deadline, SEMANTICS 6.4), built for the timeouts of PS/2 and I2C
; clock stretching, and restarts its tick generator on every edge it accepts
; (a `CSRW TICK_INT` clears ACC, SEMANTICS 4), the hard synchronisation built
; for the UART, CAN and Manchester receivers. What the firmware does that no
; hardware does: pulse-width decoding (SIRC codes the mark, where NEC codes
; the space), the windows below, the glitch filter, and finding the frame
; length (12, 15 or 20 bits) from the frame's end, since nothing on the wire
; announces it. It needs the FIFOs (PUSH) and nothing else.
;
; Pin: RX = IN0, idle high (the module's output; it has its own pull-up).
;
; Result format (FIFO space read, thread 0). Bits 15:14 give the kind:
;   frame:  two words, in the layout ir_sirc_tx.loom takes.
;           word 1  bits 6:0 the command; bits 9:8 the length: 0 = 12 bit,
;                   1 = 15, 2 = 20; bits 15:14 = 01, the rest 0.
;           word 2  12 bit: the 5-bit address; 15 bit: the 8-bit address;
;                   20 bit: the 5-bit address in 4:0, the extended byte in 15:8.
;   error:  one word, bits 15:14 = 11. Bits 12:8 n, the data bits received
;           whole before it (0..20); bit 3 in the header; bit 2 a space (0: a
;           mark); bits 1:0 the fault: 1 out of its window (too short, or
;           between two windows), 2 too long (a timeout: the line stopped
;           changing), 3 the length: the frame ended after n bits, n not 12,
;           15 or 20, or a 21st bit began (n = 20).
; Every frame is reported, repeats included: a held key gives one report per
; 45 ms, and the host, which has them all, decides what a repeat is.
; A report is never split: the program blocks on a full OUTQ, which a host
; must empty within about 4 ms (the dark time left before the next header).
; Nothing is read from INQ.
;
; Wire format: SB-Projects, "Sony SIRC protocol" (sbprojects.net/knowledge/
; ir/sirc.php) and the Linux kernel's ir-sony-decoder.c, both read
; 2026-11-05; no public Sony specification exists. Unit T = 600 us; a header
; mark of 4T and a space of T; each bit a mark of T (0) or 2T (1) and a space
; of T, least significant first; 7 command bits, then 5 address bits (12
; bit), 8 (15 bit), or 5 and 8 extended bits (20 bit). Frames start 45 ms
; apart, which leaves at least 10 T of dark time after the last mark (the
; Linux decoder's minimum trailer).
;
; Tick (set by the host before RUN, the M2 convention): one tick is T/16,
; 37.5 us.
;   50 MHz: TICK_INT 1875, TICK_FRAC 0 (exact).
;   1.92 MHz (the RTL tests): TICK_INT 72, TICK_FRAC 0 (exact).
; TICK_INT must be 64 or more (the glitch filter and the restart latency,
; below, are fractions of a tick). The program reads TICK_INT once at start.
;
; Windows, in ticks (16 to the unit) from the edge that opened the pulse:
;   header mark  4T   64 t   44 .. 84   +-25 % and 4 ticks (1.65 .. 3.15 ms)
;   header space  T   16 t    8 .. 24   (300 .. 900 us)
;   mark, a 0     T   16 t    8 .. 24
;   mark, a 1    2T   32 t   24 .. 40   (900 us .. 1.5 ms)
;   space         T   16 t    8 .. 24   another bit follows
;                             24 .. 64  an error (between the windows)
;                            64 or more the frame has ended
; The threshold between a 0 and a 1 is 1.5 T (900 us), the midpoint. The
; receiver module's error is additive, not proportional: its output pulse is
; the burst length -5/f0 .. +5/f0, +-125 us at 40 kHz (Vishay TSOP382..,
; TSOP384.. datasheet, document 82491 rev. 2.1, figure 1 note 3, the figure
; ir_nec_rx.loom uses; the 40 kHz part TSOP38240 is in that family). The
; midpoint gives both widths the same 300 us of margin, 2.4 times that error;
; the Linux decoder's windows (+-T/2 around T and 2T) meet at the same
; point. The 4 ticks (150 us) on the header are for the module too. A space
; ends the frame at 4 T (2.4 ms): longer than any space inside a frame can
; be with the module's error (T + 125 us), and well short of the 10 T dark
; time a frame is followed by, so the report goes out at least 4 ms before
; the next header can start. On an idle line a low pulse shorter than a
; header is noise and is ignored silently; every other violation ends the
; frame with an error word, and the receiver then waits for the line to be
; idle (high) again.
;
; Glitch filter, edge restarts, deadline chain and measurement error: as in
; ir_nec_rx.loom. An edge counts only if the new level is still there 2 ticks
; (75 us) later; a pulse of either level shorter than that is ignored and the
; wait it interrupted goes on against the same deadline. A confirmed edge
; restarts the tick grid, so each window is measured from the edge that
; opened it plus the filter's 2 ticks, which the deadlines take off (W =
; window - G). Each pulse is measured to within about 30 clocks (0.6 us at
; 50 MHz, 16 us at 1.92 MHz). There is no `WAITD` in the program, so the
; deadline checker has no pair to prove; `.tick` records the shortest tick
; the header allows.
;
; Registers: r0 the item being measured (error bits 3:2), then the result;
; r1 the weight of the next bit; r2 the command once its 7 bits are in; r3
; the bits being collected (the command, then the rest); r4 data bits
; received; r5 the glitch-filter delay in slots; r6 = TICK_INT for the
; restarts; r7 the delay counter and scratch.
;
; Decided at integration (2026-11-05): no public Sony specification exists;
; SB-Projects and the Linux decoder are the sources, and the 40 kHz receiver
; module's figures are taken from the 38 kHz TSOP datasheet family,
; unchecked. The transmitter sends 1 + R frames as the host asks and
; enforces no minimum (a host emulating a remote should send R >= 2). A mark
; of k units is 24k whole carrier periods, keeping mark starts on the T
; grid. The receiver ends a frame at a 4 T space, which the 45 ms grid
; leaves unambiguous.

        .imem   512                     ; the macro build: quarters of 128 words
        .thread 0
        .pins   RX = IN0
        .tick   64                      ; TICK_INT 64 or more (header)
        .equ    G     = 2               ; glitch filter, ticks
        .equ    HMMIN = 44 - G          ; header mark window, minus the filter
        .equ    HMMAX = 84 - G
        .equ    MMIN  = 8 - G           ; a T mark or space
        .equ    MMAX  = 24 - G          ; and the 0/1 threshold, 1.5 T
        .equ    M1MAX = 40 - G          ; a 2T mark
        .equ    SEND  = 64 - G          ; a space this long ends the frame
        .equ    I_HDR = 8               ; item bits of an error word
        .equ    I_SPACE = 4
        .equ    E_WIN  = 1              ; fault bits
        .equ    E_LONG = 2
        .equ    E_LEN  = 3

start:  CSRR    r6, TICK_INT            ; P, kept for the restarts
        MOV     r5, r6
        SHRI    r5, 1                   ; P / 2 slots = 2 ticks: the glitch filter,
        SUBI    r5, 4                   ; less the 4 other slots of wlow / whigh

; Hunt for a header: an idle (high) line first, then a confirmed mark.
hunt:   WAITP   RX, 1
h2:     SETD    255                     ; re-armed every 255 ticks
        CALL    wlow
        BT      h2                      ; no mark yet
        LDI     r4, 0                   ; no data bit yet: n = 0 in an error
        LDI     r0, I_HDR
        SETD    HMMIN                   ; the header mark, 44 .. 84 ticks
        CALL    whigh
        BNT     hunt                    ; too short for a header: noise, ignored
        SETD    HMMAX - HMMIN
        CALL    whigh
        BT      elong                   ; too long
        SETD    MMIN                    ; the header space, 8 .. 24 ticks
        ORI     r0, I_SPACE
        LDI     r1, 1                   ; the weight of bit 0
        LDI     r3, 0
        CALL    wlow
        BNT     ewin                    ; too short
        SETD    MMAX - MMIN
        CALL    wlow
        BT      elong                   ; too long

; A data bit's mark: 8 .. 24 ticks is a 0, 24 .. 40 a 1. Entered at its
; confirmed start.
mark:   LDI     r0, 0                   ; a mark (bits 3:2 clear)
        SETD    MMIN
        CALL    whigh
        BNT     ewin                    ; too short
        SETD    MMAX - MMIN
        CALL    whigh
        LDI     r7, 0                   ; (LDI leaves T alone)
        BNT     space                   ; a 0
        SETD    M1MAX - MMAX
        CALL    whigh
        BT      elong                   ; too long
        MOV     r7, r1                  ; a 1

; The space after it. The deadline is set first, at the mark's confirmed
; end; the bit is filed while it runs. The first 7 bits are the command.
space:  SETD    MMIN
        OR      r3, r3, r7
        SHLI    r1, 1
        INC     r4                      ; this bit is whole
        CMPI    r4, 7
        BNZ     sp1
        MOV     r2, r3                  ; the command is in; the rest from bit 0
        LDI     r3, 0
        LDI     r1, 1
sp1:    LDI     r0, I_SPACE
        CALL    wlow
        BNT     ewin                    ; too short
        SETD    MMAX - MMIN
        CALL    wlow
        BNT     sep                     ; another bit follows
        SETD    SEND - MMAX
        CALL    wlow
        BNT     ewin                    ; between the two windows
        JMP     done                    ; 4 T of dark: the frame has ended
sep:    CMPI    r4, 20
        BNZ     mark
        JMP     elen                    ; a 21st bit

; The frame has ended after r4 bits: 12, 15 or 20 is a frame.
done:   CMPI    r4, 12
        BZ      f12
        CMPI    r4, 15
        BZ      f15
        CMPI    r4, 20
        BNZ     elen
        MOV     r0, r3                  ; 20 bit: the extended byte (bits 12:5
        SHLI    r0, 3                   ; of the rest) to 15:8
        LDI     r7, 0
        LDIH    r7, 0xFF
        AND     r0, r0, r7
        ANDI    r3, 0x1F                ; the address
        OR      r3, r3, r0
        LDI     r7, 2
        JMP     rep
f15:    LDI     r7, 1
        JMP     rep
f12:    LDI     r7, 0
rep:    MOV     r0, r2                  ; the 7-bit command
        LDIH    r0, 0x40                ; a frame
        SWAP    r7, r7                  ; the length code to 9:8
        OR      r0, r0, r7
        PUSH    r0                      ; blocks while OUTQ is full
        PUSH    r3
        JMP     hunt

; Errors: r0 holds the item; n = r4 data bits were received whole.
elen:   ORI     r0, E_LEN
        JMP     err
ewin:   ORI     r0, E_WIN
        JMP     err
elong:  ORI     r0, E_LONG
err:    LDIH    r0, 0xC0
        MOV     r7, r4
        SWAP    r7, r7                  ; n to 12:8
        OR      r0, r0, r7
        PUSH    r0
        JMP     hunt                    ; which waits for an idle line first

; wlow / whigh: wait until RX is low / high or TD is reached, through the
; glitch filter. T = 1: TD came first. T = 0: the new level held for r5
; slots (2 ticks). A level that does not hold is a glitch, and the wait goes
; on against the same TD. A confirmed edge restarts the tick grid (CSRW
; TICK_INT clears ACC), exactly 2 ticks after the WAITP that saw it.
wlow:   WAITP   RX, 0, T
        BT      wlr
        MOV     r7, r5
wld:    DJNZ    r7, wld
        JP      RX, 1, wlow             ; high again: a glitch
        CSRW    TICK_INT, r6            ; the next window starts here
wlr:    RET
whigh:  WAITP   RX, 1, T
        BT      whr
        MOV     r7, r5
whd:    DJNZ    r7, whd
        JP      RX, 0, whigh            ; low again: a glitch
        CSRW    TICK_INT, r6
whr:    RET
